add(5,'Compota de Ciruelas Pasas (Cinnamon Prunes)',8,115,'Prunes are gently simmered with cinnamon, then chilled under their lightly sweetened cooking syrup.',[I('prunes',500,'g','pitted prunes','Use 500 g dried apricots for a different compote; check tenderness sooner.'),W(600),I('stick',5,'g','cinnamon sticks','Use 0.5 tsp ground cinnamon; it remains in the syrup.'),S(60)],['Put {{prunes}}, {{water}} and {{stick}} in a saucepan. Bring to a gentle simmer and cook for 15–25 minutes until plump and soft but not breaking apart.','Lift the prunes into a shallow container. Remove the cinnamon sticks and strain the cooking liquid into a clean pan.','Add {{sugar}} to the liquid and simmer for 5–10 minutes until lightly syrupy. Pour over the prunes.','Cool briefly and refrigerate within 2 hours. Chill at least 1 hour before serving.'],'Source prune, cinnamon and sugar weights are retained. Water is supplied for a practical pan rather than filling a one-liter pot to the brim. An explicitly modern light syrup replaces the ambiguous 32-degree concentration notation.')
add(2,'Spiced Crabapple Sauce for Meat',24,55,'Tart crabapples are cooked with vinegar, brown sugar and warming spices into a sweet-sour condiment.',[I('apples',454,'g','crabapples, washed, stemmed and cored','Use 454 g prepared tart cooking apples for a less sharp sauce.'),I('vinegar',480,'ml','cider vinegar labeled 5% acidity','Use the same volume of white wine vinegar for a different flavor.'),I('sugar',454,'g','brown sugar','Use 454 g granulated sugar for a lighter flavor.'),C(.67),I('cloves',.67,'tsp','ground cloves','Reduce or omit for a milder condiment.'),I('pepper',.67,'tsp','ground black pepper','Reduce or omit.'),I('salt',.67,'tsp','fine table salt','Reduce or omit; do not substitute coarse salt by equal volume.')],['Cut {{apples}} into small pieces and put in a nonreactive saucepan with {{vinegar}}. Simmer for 20–30 minutes until completely soft.','Press through a sieve into a clean pan, discarding coarse skins. Add {{sugar}}, {{cinnamon}}, {{cloves}}, {{pepper}} and {{salt}}.','Simmer gently for 5–10 minutes, stirring frequently, until the sugar dissolves and the sauce lightly coats a spoon. Add a little vinegar if it becomes too thick.','Transfer to shallow containers, cool briefly and refrigerate. Serve in small condiment portions.'],'A one-third batch retains the equal fruit-and-sugar weights. A modern 480 ml vinegar and 2/3 teaspoon spices replace uncertain quart/dessertspoon measures. Hot bottling is replaced by refrigeration; no shelf-stability is claimed. Yield is estimated condiment portions, not main servings.','Sauces')
